  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThrough Friday: Below Average Temperatures and Dry Conditions\n\nUpper level high pressure is forecast to remain over the Southern \nPlains with the main ridge axis oriented northwest towards the \nPacific Northwest. Troughing should persist over the eastern Great \nLakes leading to northwest flow over the Northern Plains into the \nMidwest. This pattern will continue to favor dry conditions along \nwith slightly below average temperatures the rest of the work\nweek. High temperatures will slowly climb back into the lower \n80s by Friday as upper level heights increase over the Southern\nPlains into the Intermountain West.\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Summer Heat Returns along with \nThunderstorm Chances\n\nThe strong high pressure system over the south central US should \nsolidly remain in place this weekend into early next week. Moisture \nthroughout the column will surge northward towards the Upper \nMississippi River Valley which should result in the return of \nthunderstorm chances. A subtle shortwave may lead to some rain \nchances on Saturday night into Sunday morning, but confidence in \nthat scenario is not high at this time with blended model guidance \ndepicting only a 10-20 percent chance at rain this far out. There is \nhigher confidence in the rain potential on Sunday afternoon into the \nevening as a stronger shortwave and secondary moisture plume move \nover the region. Sufficient instability may be in place to support a \nsevere storm threat on either Saturday or Sunday, however, low level \ncapping could be an issue due to the ridge.\n\nThe combination of higher moisture and hot temperatures may result \nin heat index values approaching 100 degrees across portions of the \nwestern and southern CWA on Sunday. The latest long range ensembles \nare certainly hinting at this potential with a 30-40 percent chance \nat reaching the 100 degree heat index value on Sunday, which is \ncurrently expected to be the hottest day of this forecast period. \nJust how hot it could get on Sunday will be highly dependent upon \ncloud cover and the potential precipitation chances throughout the \nday.",
